(3.659090909090909, 'The performance of your submission is: 3.659090909090909THOUGHT:\nThe report clearly separates host-reported measurements from proposed experiments and avoids claiming that the linear baseline establishes the complexity–regularization hypothesis. However, only one of eight candidates has a reported result, so no paired comparison or higher-degree comparison is possible. The reported remaining execution budget exceeds the seven pending evaluations, and the paper gives no explanation for stopping. All five required sections are present. The principal concern is experimental incompleteness, compounded by limited originality and missing implementation details for reproduction.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper describes a preregistered comparison of polynomial degrees 1, 2, 4, and 8, each with ridge strengths 0.0 and 0.1, using fixed partitions of 80 training and 64 validation observations. Its intended contribution is a paired complexity ladder assessing whether regularization changes the validation benefit of increasing polynomial degree. Only the unregularized degree-one configuration has a supplied result: host-reported training MSE 0.005661214895460485 and validation MSE 0.007958542384134518. The paper selects this configuration among completed candidates only and explicitly leaves the motivating hypothesis unresolved.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "The protocol specifies candidate order, fixed splits, training-only fitting, validation-based selection, and deterministic tie-breaking.",\n    "The proposed regularization, complexity, and interaction contrasts directly address the research question, and their algebraic definitions are consistent.",\n    "The report clearly distinguishes unavailable measurements from measured results and does not claim superiority over unevaluated candidates.",\n    "It appropriately distinguishes validation used for tuning from an independent test estimate and avoids treating repeated execution as independent experimental replication.",\n    "The abstract, introduction, methods, results, and discussion are all present."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central experiment is incomplete: seven of eight candidates lack results, leaving every regularization contrast and higher-degree comparison unavailable. The paper therefore provides no empirical answer to its stated research question.",\n    "The report states that 49 CPU executions remained, exceeding the seven pending evaluations, but does not explain why the preregistered search stopped. The supplied account does not establish resource exhaustion as the cause.",\n    "The empirical contribution is a single linear baseline. A conventional polynomial-ridge comparison protocol, without comparative results or a new methodological insight, is insufficient for a prestigious ML venue.",\n    "Reproducibility is limited by unspecified feature construction and scaling, intercept treatment, penalty normalization, solver details, and dependency versions. Holding these choices fixed internally does not make them reproducible externally.",\n    "The paper reports host verification and artifact availability, but does not present enough underlying evidence for readers to assess those assertions independently.",\n    "The manuscript repeatedly restates the same baseline, missing results, and interpretation caveats across sections. This substantially increases length without adding evidence.",\n    "Related work is narrow, and the extended discussion of research-agent systems contributes little to the polynomial-regression question."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 1,\n  "Quality": 2,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Why were the remaining seven preregistered candidates not evaluated when the reported execution budget could accommodate them? Was there another binding resource or workflow constraint?",\n    "Can you provide trusted-host results for the remaining candidates in the specified order, then report all available regularization, complexity, and interaction contrasts and update the selected configuration?",\n    "What exact polynomial representation, feature scaling, intercept handling, penalty normalization, numerical solver, and dependency versions does the host use?",\n    "What did independent verification check, and can the relevant execution records and fixed implementation be made available for reproduction?",\n    "What scientific insight would distinguish the completed experiment from a routine hyperparameter comparison, given its single split and two fixed penalty strengths?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The authors adequately acknowledge the incomplete search, unavailable paired comparisons, absence of independent test evaluation, and lack of uncertainty estimates.",\n    "Even a completed grid would support conclusions only about the evaluated configurations on this fixed validation split; it would not establish globally optimal degree or regularization strength.",\n    "A fixed numerical penalty across degrees can produce different shrinkage because feature scales and covariance change. Any observed interaction would require additional evidence before being attributed to a particular mechanism.",\n    "The report should explain the premature stopping and distinguish limitations of the available reporting evidence from limitations of the execution procedure.",\n    "No application-specific societal risk is established by the supplied material. A brief statement about the dataset\'s provenance and intended scope would improve the discussion of potential downstream misuse or overgeneralization."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 2,\n  "Presentation": 2,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 3,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
