(3.659090909090909, 'The performance of your submission is: 3.659090909090909THOUGHT:\nThe paper clearly distinguishes its reported baseline measurement from unperformed experiments and avoids claiming that validation error is an independent test estimate. However, its central question requires comparisons across degrees and penalties, and no such comparison is available. The reported execution allowance also appears sufficient for the seven remaining candidates, so the incomplete search needs explanation. All five required sections are present. My rejection recommendation rests on the missing empirical contribution and limited novelty, rather than on unsupported allegations of measurement fabrication.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper describes a preregistered experiment comparing polynomial regression degrees 1, 2, 4, and 8 with ridge strengths 0.0 and 0.1 on a fixed split of 80 training and 64 validation examples. It defines within-degree regularization contrasts, comparisons against linear fits, and interaction contrasts. The manuscript reports one distinct evaluated configuration, degree 1 with alpha 0.0, with training MSE 0.005661214895460485 and validation MSE 0.007958542384134518. This configuration is selected within the singleton evaluated set. The remaining seven configurations are unmeasured, leaving the motivating complexity and regularization questions unresolved.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "The planned paired complexity ladder directly separates changes in polynomial degree from changes in regularization under a fixed host implementation and validation split.",\n    "The evaluation order, selection criterion, and exact-tie rules are explicit.",\n    "The paper carefully distinguishes completed measurements from proposals and does not assign outcomes to missing configurations.",\n    "The definitions of regularization benefit, complexity benefit, and their interaction are coherent; the identity I_d = B_d - B_1 is correct.",\n    "The authors appropriately avoid interpreting the training-validation gap as proof of overfitting, repeated executions as independent samples, or tuned validation error as independent test performance.",\n    "Abstract, introduction, methods, results, and discussion sections are all present."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central empirical question is unanswered. Only one of eight configurations has a reported measurement, so there is no completed regularization pair, complexity comparison, or interaction estimate.",\n    "The manuscript reports 49 remaining CPU executions while requiring only seven additional candidate evaluations. It does not explain why the preregistered search stopped despite this apparent capacity. Available capacity does not prove that completion was operationally possible, but this discrepancy requires clarification.",\n    "Selecting the sole evaluated configuration is procedurally correct but provides no evidence supporting its predictive superiority.",\n    "Originality is limited. Polynomial degree selection, ridge regularization, and paired validation comparisons are established techniques; the report supplies neither a new estimator nor a substantive empirical finding.",\n    "The host\'s feature scaling, intercept treatment, penalty normalization, solver behavior, and dependency versions are undocumented. Keeping these choices fixed supports internal comparison, but their omission limits reproduction and interpretation of alpha 0.1 across degrees.",\n    "The single fixed split and narrow penalty grid constrain the scope of any eventual finding. Aggregate MSE values alone cannot establish uncertainty in paired loss differences or identify a covariance mechanism.",\n    "The presentation repeatedly restates the same baseline metrics and caveats across sections. This obscures the small amount of empirical evidence and makes the report substantially longer than its contribution warrants.",\n    "Related work relies on supplied synopses, includes research-agent papers peripheral to the regression question, and lacks a conventional bibliography. The relevant ridge connection is described cautiously, but the broader research context remains thin."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 1,\n  "Quality": 2,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Why were the remaining seven preregistered candidates not evaluated when the reported record listed 49 remaining CPU executions? Was there a separate resource or operational constraint?",\n    "Can the remaining candidates be submitted individually in the preregistered order and their actual host-reported validation MSE values supplied? This is the most important evidence needed to reassess the paper.",\n    "What do the two reported CPU executions represent? Please distinguish attempted execution, completed execution, duplicate evaluation, and verification without treating them as independent experimental repetitions.",\n    "Can the existing host specification document polynomial feature construction and scaling, intercept handling, penalty normalization, and numerical solver conventions without modifying the implementation?",\n    "After completing the permitted evaluations, can the authors report B_d, C_d,alpha, and I_d alongside the selected configuration, while retaining the restriction that selection covers evaluated candidates only?",\n    "What substantive insight would distinguish the completed study from a standard small hyperparameter sweep? Please identify the supported empirical conclusion without claiming unprecedented novelty or an unmeasured covariance mechanism."\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The authors adequately disclose the incomplete search, unavailable paired contrasts, absence of uncertainty estimates, and lack of independent test evaluation.",\n    "The report should more explicitly separate a verified execution claim from an independently reproducible experiment: verification is reported by the host, while the underlying artifacts were not inspected for this manuscript.",\n    "Even a completed grid would support descriptive conclusions on this fixed validation split and these eight configurations, rather than a population-level claim that complexity helps or that regularization benefit generally increases with degree.",\n    "A single positive alpha under unspecified feature scaling cannot characterize ridge regularization broadly. This should remain a scope limitation rather than motivate an unauthorized expansion of the preregistered search.",\n    "No concrete negative societal impact is apparent from the supplied regression setting. The main foreseeable reporting risk is presenting a singleton selection or tuned validation score as evidence of general predictive superiority; the manuscript largely avoids this risk."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 2,\n  "Presentation": 2,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 3,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
