   "The mathematical definitions of regularization benefit, complexity improvement, and their interaction are coherent. The discussion appropriately qualifies the background ridge identities by their assumptions.",\n    "The report avoids claiming unprecedented novelty, statistical significance, or overfitting from the training-validation error difference alone.",\n    "Abstract, introduction, methods, results, and discussion are all present."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central empirical investigation is incomplete: one of eight configurations is reported, leaving no completed regularized-unregularized pair and no higher-degree comparison. Neither motivating question can be answered.",\n    "The reported remaining allowance of 49 CPU executions appears sufficient for the seven pending candidates, but the manuscript does not explain why evaluation stopped. An incomplete search is disclosed without establishing that resource limits caused it.",\n    "The contribution is a conventional evaluation protocol and a single linear baseline. No new estimator, theoretical result, dataset, or substantive empirical conclusion supports publication at a prestigious ML venue.",\n    "Reproducibility is limited by unspecified host feature construction, scaling, intercept treatment, penalty normalization, solver behavior, and software versions. Holding these choices fixed supports internal consistency but does not enable independent reproduction.",\n    "The reported verification status and artifact list provide provenance claims within the manuscript, but the supplied review material contains no separate execution records or prediction artifacts with which to assess them independently.",\n    "The manuscript repeats the same baseline metrics and caveats across numerous sections. This repetition obscures the small amount of completed work and could be substantially condensed.",\n    "Related work relies primarily on a supplied ridge synopsis, while the research-agent papers have little bearing on the regression question. The submission does not establish a substantive advance relative to existing polynomial regression and ridge model-selection practice."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 1,\n  "Quality": 2,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Why did evaluation stop after one distinct configuration when the reported host record lists 49 remaining CPU executions? Was another binding resource limit encountered?",\n    "Can the remaining seven candidates be evaluated in preregistered order under the original fixed protocol, followed by reporting all validation MSEs, paired benefits, complexity contrasts, and the resulting selection?",\n    "Can you provide the exact host specification or a versioned implementation reference documenting feature construction, scaling, intercept handling, penalty normalization, and numerical solver behavior?",\n    "What do the two reported CPU executions represent, and what exactly did the independent verification marked valid check?",\n    "What scientific contribution beyond a local hyperparameter comparison would the completed study establish? Please distinguish conclusions about this fixed split from broader claims about complexity and regularization."\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The authors adequately acknowledge that the incomplete search leaves all central paired comparisons unavailable and that selection covers evaluated candidates only.",\n    "Even after completion, conclusions would concern one fixed split and eight preregistered candidates. Two penalty values cannot characterize ridge behavior generally, particularly when feature scales differ across degrees.",\n    "Aggregate MSE values do not support uncertainty estimates for paired loss differences. This limits interpretation but does not justify altering the mandated splits or accessing withheld test data.",\n    "The report should explain the operational reason for incomplete execution and more clearly separate limitations of the research design from missing implementation documentation.",\n    "No specific harmful application or sensitive-data use is described. A brief statement of the experiment\'s application scope and data provenance would suffice for societal-impact discussion."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 2,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 3,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True)
